1
00:00:05,269 --> 00:00:11,381
Catherine Morland has read so many spooky novels that she expects every old abbey to hide a secret.

2
00:00:11,857 --> 00:00:17,805
This guide covers Austen's comedy and a version where Catherine tells Henry about her midnight discovery.

3
00:00:18,734 --> 00:00:22,503
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

4
00:00:22,839 --> 00:00:27,313
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through Northanger Abbey, by Jane Austen.

5
00:00:29,121 --> 00:00:35,065
Catherine Morland is seventeen, a clergyman's daughter who loves thrilling Gothic novels.

6
00:00:35,397 --> 00:00:38,174
She goes to Bath with her neighbours, the Allens.

7
00:00:38,614 --> 00:00:43,744
There she meets the clever, teasing Henry Tilney and his gentle sister Eleanor.

8
00:00:44,142 --> 00:00:48,443
She also meets the boastful John Thorpe and his showy sister Isabella.

9
00:00:49,213 --> 00:00:55,317
Henry's father, General Tilney, invites Catherine to stay at Northanger Abbey, the family home.

10
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:00,711
Catherine hopes the old abbey hides dark secrets, like the castles in her books.

11
00:01:01,044 --> 00:01:04,931
Soon she starts to suspect the General of something terrible.

12
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:09,034
Next, who's who, so you can keep everyone straight.

13
00:01:10,773 --> 00:01:11,953
Catherine Morland.

14
00:01:12,095 --> 00:01:15,405
A kind, honest girl who loves Gothic novels.

15
00:01:16,186 --> 00:01:17,422
Henry Tilney.

16
00:01:17,765 --> 00:01:20,666
A witty young clergyman who loves to tease.

17
00:01:21,458 --> 00:01:22,641
Eleanor Tilney.

18
00:01:23,055 --> 00:01:26,797
Henry's gentle sister, who becomes Catherine's true friend.

19
00:01:27,498 --> 00:01:28,909
General Tilney.

20
00:01:29,242 --> 00:01:31,472
Their stern, proud father.

21
00:01:31,869 --> 00:01:33,125
Isabella Thorpe.

22
00:01:33,609 --> 00:01:36,280
Catherine's gushing new friend in Bath.

23
00:01:37,097 --> 00:01:38,097
John Thorpe.

24
00:01:38,359 --> 00:01:41,690
Isabella's brother, who brags and tells lies.

25
00:01:43,259 --> 00:01:45,147
Now for the story.

26
00:01:45,431 --> 00:01:48,418
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

27
00:01:49,293 --> 00:01:50,336
Chapter one.

28
00:01:50,583 --> 00:01:51,991
The Black Cabinet.

29
00:01:52,399 --> 00:01:58,962
Catherine Morland had read The Mysteries of Udolpho, so she knew exactly what old abbeys were like.

30
00:01:59,127 --> 00:02:04,907
They had secret passages, locked doors and mysterious papers hidden in old chests.

31
00:02:05,217 --> 00:02:11,396
So when General Tilney invited her to stay at Northanger Abbey, she could hardly breathe for excitement.

32
00:02:11,908 --> 00:02:15,677
She rode the last part of the way in Henry's open carriage.

33
00:02:15,968 --> 00:02:18,162
Henry teased her the whole way.

34
00:02:18,437 --> 00:02:25,237
He told her a spooky story about her bedroom, with a dim lamp, an old chest and an ebony cabinet.

35
00:02:25,794 --> 00:02:28,765
That night a storm howled round the abbey.

36
00:02:29,102 --> 00:02:32,399
And there in her room stood a tall black cabinet.

37
00:02:32,866 --> 00:02:35,518
Catherine's hands shook as she turned the key.

38
00:02:35,989 --> 00:02:39,601
Behind a small inner door, she found a roll of old paper.

39
00:02:40,037 --> 00:02:43,310
She trimmed her candle to see better, and it went out.

40
00:02:43,576 --> 00:02:46,435
In the morning sunshine, she unrolled the paper.

41
00:02:46,769 --> 00:02:48,048
It was a washing bill.

42
00:02:48,321 --> 00:02:50,810
Shirts, stockings and cravats.

43
00:02:51,184 --> 00:02:54,264
She went down to breakfast, red with shame.

44
00:02:54,698 --> 00:02:56,211
Henry was there alone.

45
00:02:57,048 --> 00:02:58,142
Chapter two.

46
00:02:58,391 --> 00:02:59,529
The Washing Bill.

47
00:03:00,376 --> 00:03:04,282
Henry asked, with a twinkle, if the storm had kept her awake.

48
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:07,380
Catherine was a very bad liar.

49
00:03:07,697 --> 00:03:12,087
She opened her mouth to say "a little," and found she couldn't stop there.

50
00:03:12,891 --> 00:03:14,428
"Yes," she said.

51
00:03:14,650 --> 00:03:16,456
"And it's partly your fault."

52
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:18,784
Then she told him everything.

53
00:03:18,997 --> 00:03:24,063
The cabinet, the key, the candle going out, the long night under the covers.

54
00:03:24,406 --> 00:03:29,148
She ran upstairs, fetched the papers and laid them on the table beside the hyacinths.

55
00:03:29,486 --> 00:03:31,041
Henry read the first one.

56
00:03:31,501 --> 00:03:33,771
"Shirts," he said gravely.

57
00:03:34,024 --> 00:03:35,162
"Stockings.

58
00:03:35,412 --> 00:03:37,336
A most dreadful secret."

59
00:03:38,087 --> 00:03:39,778
Catherine laughed first.

60
00:03:40,045 --> 00:03:43,660
Then Henry laughed too, so hard he had to sit down.

61
00:03:44,427 --> 00:03:46,785
"I'm sorry," he said at last.

62
00:03:47,106 --> 00:03:49,270
"I made up that cabinet to tease you.

63
00:03:49,454 --> 00:03:51,473
I never thought you'd find a real one."

64
00:03:51,822 --> 00:03:54,122
"Neither did I," said Catherine.

65
00:03:54,448 --> 00:03:59,418
Then the General came in, and they both had to look very serious about the teacups.

66
00:04:00,023 --> 00:04:03,811
After breakfast, Henry rode off to his parish at Woodston.

67
00:04:04,292 --> 00:04:05,445
Chapter three.

68
00:04:05,786 --> 00:04:06,955
The Fir Walk.

69
00:04:07,334 --> 00:04:13,598
That afternoon, Eleanor took Catherine along a narrow path through a grove of old fir trees.

70
00:04:14,282 --> 00:04:18,169
"It was my mother's favourite walk," Eleanor said quietly.

71
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:23,043
Her mother had died nine years before, when Eleanor was only thirteen.

72
00:04:23,733 --> 00:04:27,875
A day earlier, Catherine might have imagined a terrible secret.

73
00:04:28,228 --> 00:04:32,272
But she remembered the washing bill, and she simply took Eleanor's hand.

74
00:04:32,614 --> 00:04:34,734
She asked what her mother had been like.

75
00:04:35,108 --> 00:04:39,109
So Eleanor told her, and Catherine listened to every word.

76
00:04:40,066 --> 00:04:44,057
Later that week, Eleanor showed her the room where her mother had lived.

77
00:04:44,423 --> 00:04:50,041
It was bright and pleasant, with a pretty bed and the sun pouring through two tall windows.

78
00:04:50,431 --> 00:04:52,455
There was nothing strange in it at all.

79
00:04:52,831 --> 00:04:57,139
When Henry came home, Eleanor told him how kind Catherine had been.

80
00:04:57,579 --> 00:05:00,538
He looked at Catherine with something warmer than teasing.

81
00:05:01,345 --> 00:05:02,705
The Last Page.

82
00:05:03,358 --> 00:05:08,211
A few days later, the General drove them all to Woodston to see Henry's house.

83
00:05:08,968 --> 00:05:11,691
It was a cheerful home in a green village.

84
00:05:11,978 --> 00:05:16,090
A big Newfoundland puppy and some terriers came running out to meet them.

85
00:05:16,456 --> 00:05:20,500
Through the window, Catherine saw a little cottage among apple trees.

86
00:05:20,772 --> 00:05:23,947
She said it was the sweetest thing she'd ever seen.

87
00:05:24,541 --> 00:05:31,855
That evening, back at the abbey, Henry read The Mysteries of Udolpho aloud by the fire, doing all the voices.

88
00:05:32,251 --> 00:05:37,527
Catherine laughed at the scary parts, and once, when a door creaked, she laughed at herself.

89
00:05:37,846 --> 00:05:40,792
She'd come to Northanger hoping to find a mystery.

90
00:05:41,190 --> 00:05:47,273
Instead she'd found a true friend in Eleanor, and someone who laughed with her, never at her.

91
00:05:47,652 --> 00:05:50,748
And that was far better than any secret in a cabinet.

92
00:05:51,218 --> 00:05:55,634
Next, the big ideas, the ones that come up in class.

93
00:05:57,501 --> 00:05:59,536
Fiction and reality.

94
00:05:59,944 --> 00:06:03,295
Catherine learns that real life isn't a Gothic novel.

95
00:06:04,399 --> 00:06:05,399
Growing up.

96
00:06:05,711 --> 00:06:08,891
Catherine learns to trust her own judgement about people.

97
00:06:09,821 --> 00:06:11,672
True and false friends.

98
00:06:12,148 --> 00:06:17,043
Isabella's sweet words hide selfish aims, while the Tilneys are sincere.

99
00:06:17,457 --> 00:06:19,004
Money and marriage.

100
00:06:19,455 --> 00:06:23,464
The General and the Thorpes care more about fortune than feelings.

101
00:06:25,564 --> 00:06:28,397
Here are two lines worth quoting in an essay.

102
00:06:28,977 --> 00:06:35,633
"No one who had ever seen Catherine Morland in her infancy would have supposed her born to be an heroine."

103
00:06:36,310 --> 00:06:40,538
Austen jokes that Catherine seems far too ordinary to be a heroine.

104
00:06:41,385 --> 00:06:48,699
"The person, be it gentleman or lady, who has not pleasure in a good novel, must be intolerably stupid."

105
00:06:49,269 --> 00:06:53,905
Henry stands up for novels and says anyone who can't enjoy them is dull.

106
00:06:54,998 --> 00:06:57,573
Next, let's explore a what-if.

107
00:06:59,073 --> 00:07:05,788
In an alternative scenario, what if Catherine tells Henry the truth about the black cabinet?

108
00:07:06,031 --> 00:07:07,748
So what could have happened?

109
00:07:08,543 --> 00:07:14,616
Path A. Catherine hides the papers and keeps imagining mysteries, as she does in the book.

110
00:07:15,104 --> 00:07:19,476
It could happen: She's ashamed and hopes Henry never finds out.

111
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:22,400
But she can't bring herself to tell a real lie.

112
00:07:23,199 --> 00:07:27,138
Path B. Henry laughs at her, and she runs off hurt.

113
00:07:27,513 --> 00:07:30,300
It could happen: Henry loves to tease.

114
00:07:30,623 --> 00:07:33,663
But his teasing is never cruel, and he's fond of her.

115
00:07:34,089 --> 00:07:39,056
Path C. Catherine tells Henry everything, and they laugh about it together.

116
00:07:39,442 --> 00:07:43,725
This one fits the characters: Catherine is open and honest by nature.

117
00:07:43,877 --> 00:07:47,102
And Henry's jokes are always warm, not unkind.

118
00:07:47,887 --> 00:07:49,101
Which would you pick?

119
00:07:49,368 --> 00:07:52,060
Pause and decide, then see if you agree.

120
00:07:54,069 --> 00:08:00,890
The verdict: Path C. Austen's Catherine can't tell a real lie, and Henry is fond of her.

121
00:08:01,246 --> 00:08:03,457
A shared laugh is just like them.

122
00:08:05,034 --> 00:08:07,652
So what actually happens in the book?

123
00:08:07,927 --> 00:08:14,455
In the book, Catherine hides the washing bill and starts to suspect the General of harming his late wife.

124
00:08:14,883 --> 00:08:19,963
Henry catches her coming out of his mother's room and gently shows her how wrong she was.

125
00:08:20,318 --> 00:08:24,925
Soon after, the General learns she isn't rich and sends her home alone.

126
00:08:25,303 --> 00:08:30,033
But Henry follows her and proposes, and they marry within a year of first meeting.

127
00:08:30,807 --> 00:08:32,794
Why did Jane Austen choose it?

128
00:08:33,150 --> 00:08:40,700
Austen pokes fun at Gothic novels, and shows that real dangers, like greed and lies, are quieter than any ghost.

129
00:08:42,145 --> 00:08:45,593
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

130
00:08:46,557 --> 00:08:51,792
Jane Austen wrote the first version of this novel in her early twenties and called it Susan.

131
00:08:52,254 --> 00:09:00,197
A publisher bought it in 1803 for ten pounds but never printed it, so her brother Henry bought it back in 1816.

132
00:09:00,778 --> 00:09:08,243
Austen died in July 1817, aged forty-one, and the novel came out that December, together with Persuasion.

133
00:09:08,730 --> 00:09:12,817
Henry's short note inside named her as the author for the first time.

134
00:09:14,693 --> 00:09:18,259
In Austen's day, Bath was a fashionable spa town.

135
00:09:18,695 --> 00:09:23,882
Families came to drink the waters, dance at the Assembly Rooms and meet in the Pump Room.

136
00:09:24,330 --> 00:09:32,163
Gothic novels like Ann Radcliffe's The Mysteries of Udolpho were hugely popular, full of castles, secret passages and hidden

137
00:09:32,163 --> 00:09:33,171
manuscripts.

138
00:09:33,466 --> 00:09:35,911
Northanger Abbey gently makes fun of them.

139
00:09:37,834 --> 00:09:39,117
Want to see the real thing?

140
